{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ security deposit transfer and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for its return. Willfully withholding a deposit from a tenant receiving state or federal assistance may bring a $500–$2,000 penalty per offense; misuse of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense.","key_value":"$500–$2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential landlords and tenants in New Jersey; deposit disputes go to Small Claims (up to $5,000) or Special Civil Part (up to $15,000).","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500–$2,000 per offense for willfully withholding an assisted tenant's deposit; misuse of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense (fine from $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a dwelling unit only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process, and must have good cause to evict or not renew a lease. A Notice to Quit is required except for nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental dwelling units in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ rent increase notice and unconscionability rule","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease, offer a new lease at the higher rent, and give proper written notice. An increased rent must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in New Jersey; where a municipal rent control board exists, complaints go to that board.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"A rent increase must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; the exemption runs 30 years from completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings; 30-year exemption from local rent control"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years following completion; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Condominium conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership of units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rent increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"LAD source-of-lawful-income housing protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not discriminate in the rental of housing based on source of lawful income, among other protected characteristics, under the Law Against Discrimination.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex residences as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Under the LAD, violators are subject to fines up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ allowed tenant screening report and application fees","requirement":"A landlord may access prospective tenants' credit reports and charge the tenant the cost of the report, and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective residential tenants in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
